80859375, -0.0026092529296875, 0.0294342041015625, -0.0139923095703125, -0.017181396484375, -0.01174163818359375, 0.0007419586181640625, 0.027374267578125, 0.04547119140625, -0.0023193359375, 0.0021419525146484375, -0.01329803466796875, -0.014251708984375, 0.0233917236328125, -0.0200347900390625, 0.0149993896484375, 0.001033782958984375, -0.0242156982421875, -0.011138916015625, 0.0188140869140625, -0.004241943359375, 0.00031948089599609375, 0.030914306640625, -0.003936767578125, -0.017120361328125, 0.0008349418640136719, 0.00347137451171875, -0.01200103759765625, 0.0054168701171875, 0.01354217529296875, -0.0122528076171875, -0.00905609130859375, 0.0157470703125, -0.0249481201171875, -0.002887725830078125, 0.004299163818359375, -0.01045989990234375, 0.0306549072265625, -0.033111572265625, -0.0098114013671875, 0.022308349609375, 0.0141143798828125, 0.005626678466796875, -0.06005859375, -0.0230255126953125, 0.0054779052734375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) raises important questions about governance and control: Who sets direction, and when? How much autonomy should AI systems receive\u2014and through which mechanisms do humans provide guidance? This article investigates when, how, and through what mechanisms humans guide AI work, and how these patterns change depending on modes of human-AI interaction.\n\n**Key Concepts and Definitions**\n\nWe distinguish between two principal modes of delegation, each defined by the timing and extent of human input. Table 1 below summarizes definitions, boundary rules, and examples, including elaborations on gray-area cases (borderline or ambiguous scenarios where classification is less clear). For technical terms, brief explanations are provided in footnotes. (Supplementary Figure 1 in the data repository provides a detailed decision tree; Supplementary Table S1 shows a sectoral sample breakdown and includes additional borderline examples.)\n\n| Mode                 | Core Definition                                                         | Boundary Rule                                                                              | Example(s)                                                                                               |\n|----------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Specified delegation | Human sets explicit goals or constraints up front; minimal feedback after initial instructions, before output is finalized. | If substantial feedback or adjustment occurs after generation begins, not considered purely specified. All prompt engineering before initial content generation is specified. | User submits a detailed prompt (\u201cSummarize X in 200 words, covering points A, B, C\u201d) and accepts the first summary without asking for changes. Tuning or optimizing prompts before clicking 'submit' (even through trial and error) also counts as specified delegation. Editing the prompt multiple times before hitting 'submit' is still specified delegation, as long as the AI has not yet produced intermediate or final outputs. **Gray case example:** A user rewrites the prompt several times, unsure of ideal phrasing but does not view any AI output until satisfied; still classified as specified delegation. |\n| Iterative coproduction | Human collaborates interactively, providing feedback or adjustments after initial AI output. | If user reviews and revises outputs interactively after creation has begun (i.e., after any AI content is generated), it is iterative coproduction regardless of initial instructions. | User submits a prompt, receives an AI-generated draft, then asks for specific changes (\u201cExpand section 2,\u201d \u201cChange the tone to more formal\u201d) and repeats this loop. Even if the initial prompt was highly specified, any cycle of output-review-and-revise is iterative coproduction. **Gray case example:** A user accepts most of the first output but highlights a single paragraph for revision; since feedback occurs post-generation, this is iterative coproduction. Another case: the user uses \u201cregenerate\u201d after initial output to see alternatives\u2014each round is iterative coproduction. |\n\n**Resolving Gray Cases:** Any modification, experimentation, or refinement *before* the AI produces output\u2014regardless of how many times the user edits or resubmits the prompt\u2014is classified as specified delegation. All feedback, correction, or direction occurring *after* the AI generates content, whether for first or intermediate outputs, constitutes iterative coproduction.\n\n*Delegation* refers to assigning cognitive steps from human to machine. *Prior human direction* refers to human intent shaping the task before AI execution.  \n*Delegation score* refers to the normalized frequency of a given delegation mode within a defined unit of analysis (see Methodology below).  \n*Node-month-task* denotes a unique unit defined by user group (\u201cnode\u201d), calendar month, and granular task (coded at O*NET level-0\u2014a detailed occupational task classification).\n\n**Research Questions**\n\nThis study asks:  \n1. At which stages are humans most influential in shaping AI output during task delegation?  \n2. How do delegation patterns differ between structured (API-based) and interactive (chat-based) environments?\n\n**Methodology**\n\nTo ensure clarity, methodological choices are justified and technical terms explained.\n\n1. **Sample and Data Preprocessing**\n   - **Dataset:** Anthropic Economic Index (AEI), comprising 2.1 million anonymized human-AI interactions (April\u2013May 2026), with fields for interaction process, task metadata, and basic user descriptors.\n   - **Sample characteristics:** Approximately 80% U.S.-based, English-speaking knowledge workers (administration, technology, communications, business). Creative, manufacturing, and non-English tasks are underrepresented (see Supplementary Table S1 for full sectoral breakdown and gray-area task examples).\n   - **Preprocessing:** Removal of incomplete records; standardization to O*NET level-0^1^ task codes for most granular classification. Final analysis at the node-month-task^2^ level captures fine-grained usage patterns and controls for workplace seasonality or project cycles.\n\n2. **Operationalization of \"Work Orientation\"**\n   - **Definition:** Work orientation captures the proportion of a user\u2019s AI tasks flagged as work/professional rather than personal in the AEI dataset.\n   - **Measurement:** For records with both work and personal usage data, \u201cwork orientation\u201d is calculated as the percent of tasks in a given node-month marked as work-related (range: 0 to 1).\n   - **Counterfactual Modeling:** We simulate a +10 percentage point shift (e.g., from 0.40 to 0.50) in work-related intent *per node-month*, holding all else constant. This synthetic intervention is conducted on user-reported data to estimate how changes in professional orientation would affect delegation, rather than directly observing behavioral shifts. This approach allows causal inference within observational data constraints and isolates the effect of work orientation from other variables.\n   - **Rationale and Implications:** The simulation helps estimate potential impacts of future increases in professional AI use on delegation style. However, as this is a synthetic intervention, the findings reflect modeled associations, not necessarily causal behavior change, and may be influenced by unobserved factors.\n\n3. **Modeling Approach and Statistical Analysis**\n   - **Regression Model:** Linear regressions estimate the association between a modeled 10% increase in work orientation and the prevalence of each delegation mode. Node and month fixed effects are included to control for unmeasured time- or location-specific factors. Covariates for task type (O*NET level-0), user sector, and monthly usage volume are added to control for observable heterogeneity.\n   - **Sample Restriction and Justification:** Only node-month pairs with both API and Claude.ai (chat) records are included (n=1,411), providing direct within-group comparison and minimizing bias from group or organizational differences (\u201ccommon support\u201d). This restriction reduces confounding from interface self-selection (users preferring APIs may differ systematically from chat users).\n   - **Outcome Variable:** For each delegation mode, a normalized delegation score is computed per node-month-task (mean=0, SD=1), representing standardized frequency and allowing cross-mode comparison.\n   - **Robustness:** Sensitivity checks include alternative specifications (logit, including task complexity as covariate, and stratification by user AI experience), as detailed in Supplementary Table S2 and Supplementary Methods.\n   - **Statistical Reporting:** Robust standard errors are clustered by node to account for within-group correlation. Full regression tables and robustness checks are summarized in Appendix Table A1.\n\n*Table A1: Key Regression Model Parameters (see Appendix for full details)*  \n| Variable                | Coefficient (API) | Coefficient (Claude.ai) | 95% CI              | Covariates        |\n|-------------------------|-------------------|-------------------------|---------------------|-------------------|\n| Work orientation (+10%) | 2.76 (specified), -0.30 (iterative) | 1.45 (specified), +0.15 (iterative) | [2.30, 3.22], etc. | Task type, sector, volume, FE |\n\n**Findings and Interpretation**\n\n_Empirical Results:_\n- When work orientation is synthetically increased by 10 percentage points (from observed user data), **specified delegation** rises by 2.76 standard deviations (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) in API environments and by 1.45 (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]) in Claude.ai (chat). This equates to a practical jump in the share of \u201chighly specified up-front instructions\u201d from a median (~40% of tasks) to approximately 85%.\n- **Iterative coproduction** decreases in API mode by 0.30 standard deviations (SD) but increases slightly in Claude.ai by 0.15 SD. The difference in effect size between interfaces is statistically significant (gap: 0.45 SD; 95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]).\n- Subgroup analyses (Supplementary Table S2) show that administration and business users see larger increases in specified delegation, while creative and communication sectors show modest gains in iterative coproduction via chat.\n- **Gray Cases & Task Complexity:** Sensitivity analyses stratified by user AI experience and task complexity find consistent patterns, with less experienced users\u2014and those facing highly ambiguous tasks\u2014leaning more toward iterative coproduction regardless of interface, though the overall magnitudes differ.\n\n_Interpretation and Implications:_\n- These results suggest that in professionalized, work-oriented contexts, APIs tend to channel users toward up-front, structured specification\u2014encouraging a \u201cset and execute\u201d model with limited feedback cycles. In contrast, chat modes (e.g., Claude.ai) support ongoing collaboration, re-specification, and real-time oversight, especially with open-ended or creative tasks.\n- Statistical significance is high for specified delegation increases in both modes, but only chat interfaces show a positive (if modest) increase in iterative coproduction. Practical implications include the potential for mismatched user expectations if delegation model and interface do not align.\n\n**Limitations and Boundary Conditions**\n\nSeveral constraints should be noted:\n\n- **Sample Representativeness:** The dataset is skewed toward U.S.-based, English-speaking, office professionals, limiting generalizability to other populations or"}}